glthread: do not dereference an upload buffer that could not be allocated

_mesa_glthread_upload() took the result of new_upload_buffer() for the
shared 1 MB upload buffer and went straight on to
"glthread->upload_buffer->RefCount += default_size". new_upload_buffer()
returns NULL when the buffer's storage or its CPU mapping cannot be had,
which the winsys reports as soon as it has no memory left to give. The
program then died with a bad access at address 0 inside glDrawRangeElements
(Unreal Tournament 2004 on the G5, 2026-10-10).

Return instead, with *out_buffer left NULL. Every caller already tests that
and raises GL_OUT_OF_MEMORY, so the draw is skipped and the program goes on.
The shared buffer stays NULL, so the next upload tries again.

diff -ruN a/src/mesa/main/glthread_bufferobj.c b/src/mesa/main/glthread_bufferobj.c
--- a/src/mesa/main/glthread_bufferobj.c
+++ b/src/mesa/main/glthread_bufferobj.c
@@ -131,6 +131,15 @@

       glthread->upload_buffer =
          new_upload_buffer(ctx, default_size, &glthread->upload_ptr);
+
+      /* osx-gpu: no memory. *out_buffer stays NULL; callers report
+       * GL_OUT_OF_MEMORY and skip the draw. */
+      if (unlikely(!glthread->upload_buffer)) {
+         glthread->upload_ptr = NULL;
+         glthread->upload_offset = 0;
+         return;
+      }
+
       glthread->upload_offset = 0;
       offset = start_offset;

